WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.525
Output requirements make responses easier to review, reuse, or process.

2
00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:10.861
Useful formats include headings, bullet lists, tables, labeled fields, checklists, templates, or JSON-like structures.

3
00:00:10.861 --> 00:00:18.102
A support workflow may need fields for issue type, severity, customer impact, and suggested next action.

4
00:00:18.102 --> 00:00:23.532
A writing workflow may need a title, summary, assumptions, and revision notes.

5
00:00:23.532 --> 00:00:28.963
Structure helps humans scan results and helps software route or validate them.

6
00:00:28.963 --> 00:00:32.131
The format should fit the downstream use.

7
00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:38.467
If a human will review the answer, clarity may matter more than strict syntax.

8
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:46.612
If software will parse the result, the format should be simple and the application should still validate it.

9
00:00:46.612 --> 00:00:52.043
A model can produce malformed or unexpected output even when instructed carefully.

10
00:00:52.043 --> 00:00:57.021
Deterministic validation belongs in the application, not only in the prompt.

11
00:00:57.021 --> 00:00:58.379
Examples shape behavior.

12
00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:01.094
Zero-shot prompting gives instructions without examples.

13
00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:03.357
One-shot prompting gives one example.

14
00:01:03.357 --> 00:01:06.072
Few-shot prompting gives several representative examples.

15
00:01:06.072 --> 00:01:11.955
Examples can demonstrate labels, style, level of detail, transformations, edge-case handling, or formatting.

16
00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:16.028
For classification, examples can clarify the boundary between categories.

17
00:01:16.028 --> 00:01:21.006
For data extraction, examples can show how to handle missing fields.

18
00:01:21.006 --> 00:01:22.816
Examples also carry risk.

19
00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:27.342
Poor examples can introduce bias, accidental constraints, or contradictory patterns.

20
00:01:27.342 --> 00:01:35.488
If every example is short, the model may produce short answers even when a longer answer is needed.

21
00:01:35.488 --> 00:01:41.371
If examples ignore edge cases, the prompt may perform poorly on real inputs.

22
00:01:41.371 --> 00:01:46.349
Examples should represent ordinary cases and difficult cases when reliability matters.

23
00:01:46.349 --> 00:01:54.042
Treat examples as training signals inside the prompt, and test their effects rather than assuming they help.

24
00:01:54.042 --> 00:01:57.210
For machine-consumed output, keep the schema modest.

25
00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:04.451
A huge nested structure may look precise but be harder for the model to produce reliably.

26
00:02:04.451 --> 00:02:10.334
Prefer clear field names, simple value rules, and explicit handling for unknown information.

27
00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:18.480
If the downstream system needs strict JSON, the application should parse and validate the output, then handle errors.

28
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:24.815
Prompt wording can reduce format failures, but it should not be the only control.
